WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:14.628
OLIVIA: Welcome back to People in the News , Little Fox's biography show. This week we’ll be

2
00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:19.608
talking about American talk show host Oprah Winfrey. She's one of the richest and most successful

3
00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:24.112
women in the world today. But Oprah wasn't born wealthy, was she, Ben? BEN: No, Olivia. Oprah

4
00:00:24.113 --> 00:00:27.703
was born into poverty in 1954. She spent her first six years living on a farm

5
00:00:27.704 --> 00:00:32.270
with her grandmother, Hattie Mae. The farm was very isolated, so Oprah's only companions were the farm

6
00:00:32.271 --> 00:00:36.848
animals and a doll that her grandmother had made from a corncob. Despite not having any

7
00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:45.103
money, Hattie Mae was able to introduce Oprah to books, teaching her to read at a young

8
00:00:45.104 --> 00:00:54.844
age and instilling a strong love of learning. By the time Oprah was three years old,

9
00:00:54.845 --> 00:01:00.913
she was memorizing speeches and performing them at church. People were amazed by the little girl's talent

10
00:01:00.914 --> 00:01:04.750
for public speaking. But life changed for Oprah when she was six. She went to live

11
00:01:04.751 --> 00:01:07.929
with her mother, who didn't place much value on education. She complained when Oprah read books, and

12
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:12.489
she accused Oprah of thinking she was better than other kids. Suddenly Oprah was being told

13
00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:18.565
that she shouldn't be smart, and this was very confusing to her. But Oprah's interest in learning

14
00:01:18.566 --> 00:01:24.581
couldn't be suppressed. When she started kindergarten, she quickly realized that she was brighter than many

15
00:01:24.582 --> 00:01:27.982
of the other students. So she wrote a note to her teacher saying she was too smart

16
00:01:27.983 --> 00:01:34.137
for kindergarten. The teacher agreed, and the next day Oprah was moved up to first grade!

17
00:01:34.138 --> 00:01:38.467
Oprah eventually went to live with her father, who insisted that Oprah get good grades. Another important

18
00:01:38.468 --> 00:01:42.965
influence was her fourth-grade teacher, Mrs. Duncan, who encouraged Oprah to read a lot and often

19
00:01:42.966 --> 00:01:48.374
stayed after school to help her with schoolwork. Most importantly, though, Mrs. Duncan taught Oprah that, despite

20
00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:57.449
what anyone said, Oprah should be proud of herself for being smart. She's never forgotten that

21
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:05.616
important lesson. After winning a beauty contest when she was 17, Oprah got a job as a

22
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:08.621
newscaster at a radio station. During her sophomore year of college, a local television station offered

23
00:02:08.622 --> 00:02:15.690
her a job as a news anchor. Oprah was only 19, and she was already on TV!

24
00:02:15.691 --> 00:02:21.435
That was a difficult time for Oprah though—she was starting her television career while still going

25
00:02:21.436 --> 00:02:25.844
to college. She went to classes in the morning and then worked at the station until ten

26
00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:32.185
o'clock at night. She often stayed up until two or three in the morning, studying and

27
00:02:32.186 --> 00:02:37.500
doing homework. OLIVIA: How did Oprah go from being a news anchor to having her own talk

28
00:02:37.501 --> 00:02:42.918
show? BEN: Well, Oprah wasn't a very good news reporter. When she was talking about tragedies,

29
00:02:42.919 --> 00:02:47.827
crimes, and other events, she often got upset and showed too much emotion. So the station moved

30
00:02:47.828 --> 00:02:51.323
her to an early morning talk show where she could be herself and interview guests in

31
00:02:51.324 --> 00:02:58.999
a more relaxed atmosphere. After filming her first episode, Oprah knew right away that hosting a talk

32
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:06.139
show was what she wanted to do for her career. In 1984 Oprah moved to Chicago

33
00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:13.147
to host another talk show. Oprah did really well, and the show's name was soon changed to

34
00:03:13.148 --> 00:03:17.463
The Oprah Winfrey Show . Within a few years, it was the most popular talk show

35
00:03:17.464 --> 00:03:22.968
in America, eventually turning Oprah into a billionaire, as well as one of the most influential people

36
00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:32.628
in the world. The Oprah Winfrey Show ended in 2011. While it was on the air,

37
00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:40.542
it featured interviews with many famous people. But it also broadcast many inspiring stories about ordinary people

38
00:03:40.543 --> 00:03:48.788
who had dealt with tragedies or overcome challenging circumstances. Oprah often used her show to talk

39
00:03:48.789 --> 00:03:55.280
about important social issues such as race, child abuse, and AIDS. She was open to talking about

40
00:03:55.281 --> 00:04:01.762
her own life too, which was a big reason for her popularity with TV viewers. In

41
00:04:01.763 --> 00:04:06.514
one famous episode, she invited her former teacher, Mrs. Duncan, on the show. Many years had gone

42
00:04:06.515 --> 00:04:12.933
by, but now Oprah could tell her favorite teacher just how important she had been to

43
00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:15.858
Oprah. It was a special moment for both women. Oprah's show also provided an opportunity for her

44
00:04:15.859 --> 00:04:21.092
to share her love of books. She started a segment called "Oprah's Book Club," in which

45
00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:27.488
she selected a new book to read each month. Audience members read the book along with her,

46
00:04:27.489 --> 00:04:34.662
and at the end of the month, Oprah invited the book's author to appear on the

47
00:04:34.663 --> 00:04:41.480
show. Most of the books Oprah chose became instant bestsellers, and her segment encouraged millions of people

48
00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:44.317
to read. Oprah is also passionate about acting. In 1986 she was nominated for an Academy

49
00:04:44.318 --> 00:04:52.316
Award—an important film industry award—for her role in the movie The Color Purple . Her interest in

50
00:04:52.317 --> 00:05:00.476
movies led her to start her own production company, HARPO Productions, which has made several movies

51
00:05:00.477 --> 00:05:04.051
based on books Oprah likes. But Oprah isn't only involved in the entertainment business. Over the years

52
00:05:04.052 --> 00:05:10.223
she has frequently used her enormous wealth and influence to do important charity work. In 1998

53
00:05:10.224 --> 00:05:19.362
she created Oprah's Angel Network, a charitable organization that encourages people to volunteer and help others who

54
00:05:19.363 --> 00:05:27.535
are less fortunate. Oprah's Angel Network has helped to build homes and schools around the world,

55
00:05:27.536 --> 00:05:35.107
as well as provide books and school supplies for disadvantaged students. In 2007 Oprah established a new

56
00:05:35.108 --> 00:05:39.104
boarding school in South Africa, the Oprah Winfrey Leadership Academy for Girls. Oprah is very involved

57
00:05:39.105 --> 00:05:44.608
with this school for academically gifted girls. And guess what, Olivia, she even picked out the girls'

58
00:05:44.609 --> 00:05:48.270
bed sheets! OLIVIA: Thanks for the interesting report, Ben. It sounds like Oprah has never forgotten

59
00:05:48.271 --> 00:05:55.832
her own poor childhood and the important role that education played in her success. Well, that's it

60
00:05:55.833 --> 00:06:06.404
for this edition of People in the News . See you next week, Little Fox readers!

